= Thinking Like a Mountain / 像山一样思考

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("A deep chesty bawl echoes from rimrock to rimrock, rolls down the mountain, and fades into the far blackness of the night. It is an outburst of wild defiant sorrow, and of contempt for all the adversities of the world. Every living thing (and perhaps many a dead one as well) pays heed to that call. To the deer it is a reminder of the way of all flesh, to the pine a forecast of midnight scuffles and of blood upon the snow, to the coyote a promise of gleanings to come, to the cowman a threat of red ink at the bank, to the hunter a challenge of fang against bullet. Yet behind these obvious and immediate hopes and fears there lies a deeper meaning, known only to the mountain itself. Only the mountain has lived long enough to listen objectively to the howl of a wolf.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("一声低沉而浑厚的嚎叫，在峭壁之间回荡，滚落山坡，消失在夜色遥远的黑暗里。那是狂野而倔强的悲哀迸发出来的声音，也是对世间一切逆境的蔑视。每一个活物——也许还有许多死物——都在倾听。对鹿来说，它提醒着一切血肉终将遭遇的命运；对松树来说，它预告着午夜的搏斗和雪地上的鲜血；对郊狼来说，它许诺了将来的残羹；对牧牛人来说，它意味着银行账上的赤字；对猎人来说，它是一场利齿与子弹之间的挑战。然而，在这些显而易见、迫在眉睫的希望和恐惧背后，还藏着更深的意义，唯有山自己知道。只有山活得够久，能够客观地倾听狼嚎。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("Those unable to decipher the hidden meaning know nevertheless that it is there, for it is felt in all wolf country, and distinguishes that country from all other land. It tingles in the spine of all who hear wolves by night, or who scan their tracks by day. Even without sight or sound of wolf, it is implicit in a hundred small events: the midnight whinny of a pack horse, the rattle of rolling rocks, the bound of a fleeing deer, the way shadows lie under the spruces. Only the ineducable tyro can fail to sense the presence or absence of wolves, or the fact that mountains have a secret opinion about them.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("即使不能解读那隐秘的意义，人们也知道它在那里：在所有狼出没的地方都能感觉到，它使那片土地有别于其他地方。夜里听见狼嚎，白天寻找狼迹，人的脊背都会泛起一阵战栗。即使看不见狼，也听不到狼，它仍隐含在无数小事里：驮马午夜的嘶鸣，滚落石块的碰撞声，逃跑的鹿纵身一跃，云杉下面阴影铺展的样子。只有怎么也教不会的新手，才感觉不到狼的存在或缺席，也感觉不到山对狼怀有一个秘密的看法。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("My own conviction on this score dates from the day I saw a wolf die. We were eating lunch on a high rimrock, at the foot of which a turbulent river elbowed its way. We saw what we thought was a doe fording the torrent, her breast awash in white water. When she climbed the bank toward us and shook out her tail, we realized our error: it was a wolf. A half‐dozen others, evidently grown pups, sprang from the willows and all joined in a welcoming melee of wagging tails and playful maulings. What was literally a pile of wolves writhed and tumbled in the center of an open flat at the foot of our rimrock.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("我对此的确信，始于亲眼看见一只狼死去的那一天。我们在高高的岩壁上吃午饭，脚下一条湍急的河流曲折奔涌。我们看见一个身影涉水渡河，胸前激起白色的浪花，以为那是一只母鹿。等它朝我们爬上河岸，抖开尾巴，我们才发现认错了：那是一只狼。另有五六只狼，显然是已长大的幼狼，从柳丛中跃出，摇着尾巴、嬉闹撕咬，汇成一团欢迎它。在岩壁下方的开阔平地中央，简直是一堆狼在翻滚、扭动。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("In those days we had never heard of passing up a chance to kill a wolf. In a second we were pumping lead into the pack, but with more excitement than accuracy: how to aim a steep downhill shot is always confusing. When our rifles were empty, the old wolf was down, and a pup was dragging a leg into impassable slide‐rocks.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("那时候，我们从未听说有人会放过杀狼的机会。一瞬间，我们就朝狼群倾泻子弹，激动有余，准头不足：陡坡上向下射击，总让人拿不准怎样瞄准。等枪里的子弹打光，老狼已经倒下，一只幼狼拖着一条腿，躲进无法通行的碎石坡。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("We reached the old wolf in time to watch a fierce green fire dying in her eyes. I realized then, and have known ever since, that there was something new to me in those eyes ‐ something known only to her and to the mountain. I was young then, and full of trigger‐itch; I thought that because fewer wolves meant more deer, that no wolves would mean hunters' paradise. But after seeing the green fire die, I sensed that neither the wolf nor the mountain agreed with such a view.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("我们赶到老狼身边，来得及看见一团炽烈的绿火从它眼中熄灭。就在那时，我意识到那双眼睛里有一种我从未了解的东西，只有它和山知道；此后，我一直明白这一点。当时我很年轻，满心想扣动扳机。我想，既然狼少就意味着鹿多，那么没有狼，就该是猎人的天堂。但看过那绿火熄灭之后，我感觉到，狼和山都不赞同这种看法。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("Since then I have lived to see state after state extirpate its wolves. I have watched the face of many a newly wolfless mountain, and seen the south-facing slopes wrinkle with a maze of new deer trails. I have seen every edible bush and seedling browsed, first to anaemic desuetude, and then to death. I have seen every edible tree defoliated to the height of a saddlehorn. Such a mountain looks as if someone had given God a new pruning shears, and forbidden Him all other exercise. In the end the starved bones of the hoped‐for deer herd, dead of its own too‐much, bleach with the bones of the dead sage, or molder under the high‐lined junipers.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("从那以后，我亲历了一个又一个州把狼赶尽杀绝。我看着许多刚刚失去狼的山，南坡被纵横交错的新鹿径划得满是皱纹。我看见每一丛可食的灌木和每一株幼苗被啃食，先衰弱、枯萎，最后死去。我看见每一棵可食的树，在马鞍鞍角的高度以下都被吃光叶子。那样的山，像是有人给了上帝一把新的修枝剪，却不许祂干别的事。到头来，那些曾被盼望着增多的鹿群，因为数量过多而饿死，它们的骨头，与死去的鼠尾草一起曝晒发白，或在枝叶高悬的杜松下面腐朽。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("I now suspect that just as a deer herd lives in mortal fear of its wolves, so does a mountain live in mortal fear of its deer. And perhaps with better cause, for while a buck pulled down by wolves can be replaced in two or three years, a range pulled down by too many deer may fail of replacement in as many decades. So also with cows. The cowman who cleans his range of wolves does not realize that he is taking over the wolf's job of trimming the herd to fit the range. He has not learned to think like a mountain. Hence we have dustbowls, and rivers washing the future into the sea.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("如今我怀疑，正如鹿群极度害怕狼，山也极度害怕鹿，而且也许更有理由。一只被狼扑倒的雄鹿，两三年就能补上；一片被过多的鹿毁掉的牧场，几十年也未必能恢复。牛也是如此。把牧场上的狼清除干净的牧牛人，没有意识到，自己正在接手狼的工作：削减兽群，使它的数量适合牧场。他还没有学会像山一样思考。所以，我们才有了尘暴，有了把未来冲进大海的河流。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("We all strive for safety, prosperity, comfort, long life, and dullness. The deer strives with his supple legs, the cowman with trap and poison, the statesman with pen, the most of us with machines, votes, and dollars, but it all comes to the same thing: peace in our time. A measure of success in this is all well enough, and perhaps is a requisite to objective thinking, but too much safety seems to yield only danger in the long run. Perhaps this is behind Thoreau's dictum: In wildness is the salvation of the world. Perhaps this is the hidden meaning in the howl of the wolf, long known among mountains, but seldom perceived among men.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("我们都在追求安全、繁荣、舒适、长寿和无聊。鹿靠着灵巧的腿，牧牛人靠陷阱和毒药，政治家靠笔，而我们大多数人靠机器、选票和金钱；归根结底，都是为了同一个目标：让我们所处的时代安宁。取得适度的成功当然不错，甚至也许是客观思考的必要条件；然而，从长远看，过度的安全似乎只会带来危险。梭罗那句‘世界的救赎在于野性’，背后也许就是这个道理。也许这就是狼嚎隐藏的意义，群山早已知晓，人却很少领会。")]
